---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import Figure from "../components/Figure.astro";
import { CLUB_RECORDS_URL } from "../consts";

const AGB_CLASSIFICATIONS =
	"https://archerygb.org/resources/find-a-document/awards/outdoor-classifications-and-handicaps";
const AGB_PROGRESS = "https://archerygb.org/clubs/badges-and-awards/progress-awards-scheme";
---

<Page
	title="Archery info"
	description="How handicaps, classifications and badges work in archery, plus a link to our club scores."
	intro="New to the detail of the sport? Here's how handicaps, classifications and badges work — and where to see club scores."
>
	<section id="handicaps" class="has-figure">
		<h2>How handicaps work</h2>
		<Figure
			src="/images/community/target-detail.jpg"
			alt="A close-up of arrows grouped in the gold of a target face"
			caption="Chasing that elusive gold."
			align="right"
		/>
		<p>
				Your handicap is a measure of how well you've shot, regardless of the round, the distances
				or the number of arrows. It runs from 0 (the best) to 150 (the worst). It's a logarithmic
				scale: each 1-point reduction means shooting about 3.5% more accurately.
			</p>
			<p>
				Every archer gets a handicap, much like in golf. It starts as the average of your first
				three rounds, rounded down to a whole number. After that, if you shoot better than your
				handicap it's lowered to the midpoint between your current handicap and the round just shot
				(rounded down); if you shoot worse, it stays the same. There are separate handicaps for
				indoors and outdoors, and for each bow type.
			</p>
			<p>
				At the end of a season, handicaps are reassessed by averaging your three best, rounded up —
				so your handicap can go up or down compared with your running one.
			</p>
			<p>
				Handicaps are used in some competitions so everyone has an equal chance of winning. We
				usually hold an end-of-season indoor Worcester and an outdoor Nationals competition in
				September, both handicap events: an adjustment based on your handicap is added to your score,
				and higher (worse) handicaps get a larger adjustment. Shoot a round at exactly your current
				handicap and you'll finish on 1440.
			</p>
	</section>

	<section id="classifications">
		<h2>Classifications</h2>
			<p>Indoors, classifications run from H (lowest) to A (highest).</p>
			<p>
				Outdoors, they begin at <strong>Archer</strong> tier (3rd, 2nd and 1st class), progress to
				<strong>Bowman</strong> tier (3rd, 2nd and 1st class), and finish in the
				<strong>Master Bowman</strong> tier: Master Bowman (MB), then Grand Master Bowman (GMB), and
				finally Elite Master Bowman (EMB).
			</p>
			<p>
				Archer and Bowman classifications can be earned at any club-level event; Master Bowman
				classifications must be shot at a record-status competition.
			</p>
			<p>
				Scores are based on handicap levels, so a given classification takes the same skill
				whatever round you shoot — with different scores for different bow types, ages and genders.
			</p>
			<a class="btn" href={AGB_CLASSIFICATIONS} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
				>Archery GB classification tables</a
			>
	</section>

	<section id="badges">
		<h2>Badges</h2>
			<p>
				Badges are awarded for classifications, both indoors and outdoors. Archer and Bowman tier
				badges are awarded at club level; Master Bowman tier badges are awarded by Archery GB.
			</p>
			<h3>Portsmouth badges (indoor)</h3>
			<p>
				Start at a score of 500 for seniors, and 300 for juniors, longbows and barebows. One
				qualifying score on a Portsmouth round earns the badge. They then rise in 25-point steps to
				575, and in 5-point steps to the maximum score of 600.
			</p>
			<h3>252 badges (outdoor, or indoor at 20 yards)</h3>
			<p>
				You need a score of 252 twice, on different days, using imperial 5-zone scoring on a 122 cm
				face, with the day's first 3 dozen arrows shot at the chosen distance. Distances range from
				20 to 100 yards. (3 dozen is for recurve; barebow 4 dozen, longbow 4½ dozen, compound 2¾
				dozen.) These arrows can be part of a normal round — for example, shoot a Short National to
				go for the 50-yard 252.
			</p>
			<p class="note">
				If you shoot a 252, highlight it on your score sheet for your first and second scores, and
				let the records officer know.
			</p>
	</section>

	<section id="junior-awards">
		<h2>Junior progress awards</h2>
		<p>
			Archery GB's Progress Award Scheme offers progress badges for juniors — white, black, blue,
			red and gold.
		</p>
		<a class="btn" href={AGB_PROGRESS} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
			>Archery GB Progress Award Scheme</a
		>
	</section>

	<section id="rounds">
		<h2>Archery rounds</h2>
		<p>
			A plain-English guide to the common rounds — distances, arrow counts, target faces and
			scoring, from Portsmouth and Bray indoors to National, Western and York/Hereford outdoors —
			is coming soon.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="scores" class="has-figure">
		<h2>Club scores &amp; records</h2>
		<Figure
			src="/images/community/on-the-line.jpg"
			alt="Aequus Archers members shooting together on the line"
			caption="Members on the line during a club shoot."
			align="left"
		/>
		<p>Members can view recorded scores, handicaps and club records on our scores site.</p>
		<a class="btn" href={CLUB_RECORDS_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">View club scores</a>
	</section>
</Page>
